# Store every text file with LF and check it out with LF, on every machine, regardless
# of that machine's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true converts to CRLF on checkout while the repo itself
# holds LF. Three consequences, all of which we hit:
#
#   1. Every `git add` prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches
#      it" for most files -- constant noise that trains you to ignore git's output.
#   2. A fresh clone's working tree differs from an older one by line ending alone, so
#      deploying from a fresh clone rewrote ~8 files it should have reported as [SAME].
#      That makes [SAME] useless as a signal for whether anything really changed.
#   3. Any byte-for-byte comparison between two clones reports total drift. publish.ps1
#      normalizes before comparing for exactly this reason.
#
# LF is safe for everything in this repo: Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7 both
# parse LF-only scripts, and the .ps1 files here were already LF.
* text=auto eol=lf
